  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n6 0 6\n0 6 6\n6 0 6\n4 0 4\n0 4 4\n4 0 4\n8 8 8\n8 0 8\n8 8 8\nOutput:\n8 8 8\n8 0 8\n8 8 8\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n2 0 0 3 0 0 7 0 7 1 0 0\n2 0 0 3 0 0 0 7 0 1 0 0\n0 2 2 0 3 3 7 0 7 0 1 1\nOutput:\n7 0 7\n0 7 0\n7 0 7\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n3 0 0 4 0 4 2 0 0 8 0 0 1 0 0\n0 3 3 4 4 4 0 2 2 0 8 8 0 1 1\n0 3 0 4 0 4 0 2 0 0 8 0 0 1 0\nOutput:\n4 0 4\n4 4 4\n4 0 4\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n0 7 7\n7 7 0\n7 0 7\n3 0 0\n0 3 3\n3 0 0\n2 0 0\n0 2 2\n2 0 0\n8 0 0\n0 8 8\n8 0 0\nOutput:\n0 7 7\n7 7 0\n7 0 7\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n0 5 0\n5 0 5\n0 5 0\n0 3 0\n3 0 3\n0 3 0\n6 0 6\n6 6 0\n6 0 6\n0 4 0\n4 0 4\n0 4 0\n0 8 0\n8 0 8\n0 8 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n    \n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows > 0 else 0\n    \n    blocks = []\n    patterns = []\n    \n    # Extract all 3x3 blocks\n    for i in range(0, rows, 3):\n        for j in range(0, cols, 3):\n            # Extract 3x3 block\n            block = []\n            for r in range(i, min(i+3, rows)):\n                row = []\n                for c in range(j, min(j+3, cols)):\n                    row.append(grid[r][c])\n                block.append(row)\n            \n            # Normalize pattern: replace non-zero with 1, zero stays 0\n            pattern = tuple(\n                tuple(1 if cell != 0 else 0 for cell in row)\n                for row in block\n            )\n            \n            blocks.append(block)\n            patterns.append(pattern)\n    \n    # Find the pattern that appears exactly once\n    from collections import Counter\n    pattern_counts = Counter(patterns)\n    \n    for idx, pattern in enumerate(patterns):\n        if pattern_counts[pattern] == 1:\n            return blocks[idx]\n    \n    # Fallback: return first block if nothing is unique\n    return blocks[0] if blocks else []\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n    \n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows > 0 else 0\n    \n    blocks = []\n    patterns = []\n    \n    # Extract all 3x3 blocks\n    for i in range(0, rows, 3):\n        for j in range(0, cols, 3):\n            # Extract 3x3 block\n            block = []\n            for r in range(i, min(i+3, rows)):\n                row = []\n                for c in range(j, min(j+3, cols)):\n                    row.append(grid[r][c])\n                block.append(row)\n            \n            # Normalize pattern: replace non-zero with 1, zero stays 0\n            pattern = tuple(\n                tuple(1 if cell != 0 else 0 for cell in row)\n                for row in block\n            )\n            \n            blocks.append(block)\n            patterns.append(pattern)\n    \n    # Find the pattern that appears exactly once\n    from collections import Counter\n    pattern_counts = Counter(patterns)\n    \n    for idx, pattern in enumerate(patterns):\n        if pattern_counts[pattern] == 1:\n            return blocks[idx]\n    \n    # Fallback: return first block if nothing is unique\n    return blocks[0] if blocks else []",
